👉 Task 6. . Changing Permissions
Task 6.1: Create the File
Command: touch secure.txt
Explanation:The touch command creates an empty file named secure.txt in your current directory if it does not already exist. If it already exists, touch updates its timestamps without clearing its contents.

Task 6.2: Set Read-Only Permissions
Command: chmod 444 secure.txt
Explanation:The chmod command changes file permissions. The value 444 gives read permission only to the owner, group, and all other users, removing their write and execute permissions.

👉 Task 7. Working with Environment Variables
Task 7.1: Set the Environment Variable
Command: export MY_VAR='Hello, Linux!'
Explanation: export makes the variable available to programs and child processes launched from the current shell.
MY_VAR is the variable’s name.
'Hello, Linux!' is its value. Use straight single quotes as shown to preserve the spaces and exclamation mark literally.
Do not add spaces around the = sign.

Task 7.2: Verify the Variable
Command:printenv MY_VAR
Expected Output:Hello, Linux!
The printenv command displays the value of the specified environment variable, confirming that it was exported successfully
